2. Costa Rican transmission network — `cr6-*`, `cr8-*`, `ice-*` corpus
   instances derived from ICE open data (the verbatim source no longer ships)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Source: Instituto Costarricense de Electricidad (ICE), open-data portal
`datos-ice-se.opendata.arcgis.com` — datasets `Subestaciones` and
`LineasDeTransmision`.

Verified against the portal's DCAT feed on 2026-08-06:

  * publisher: ICE (Instituto Costarricense de Electricidad)
  * accessLevel: "public" for every dataset in the catalog, these two included
  * a license FIELD exists for both datasets, but the catalog declares no
    standard license identifier (no CC-BY, no ODbL, no public-domain
    dedication). One sibling dataset carries the free-text value "Uso Público".

**Open question, deliberately not papered over.** "Open data, public access"
is not the same as an explicit grant to redistribute derived works. Attribution
credits the source; it does not authorize redistribution on its own. That
question is still open for the bytes described in the last paragraph below
(test fixture data); it is the reason the verbatim portal copy that used to
ship in this tree was removed.

**What no longer ships (decided 2026-08-10/11, #173.1, option b of the
plataforma handoff §1.1 — executed):** `knowledge/islanding/raw/` and the two
files it held, `ice-subestaciones.geojson` / `ice-lineas-transmision.geojson`
— the actual verbatim copy of the portal's datasets — have been deleted from
the tree. `scripts/gen_corpus_ice.py` can no longer run against a fresh
checkout; it fails loud with download instructions instead of a bare
`FileNotFoundError`. The derivation recipe and every digest it produces stay
in the script and in the corpus below, so anyone who obtains the portal data
independently can point the script at their own copy (`--raw-dir` /
`$CHIMERA_ICE_RAW_DIR`) and re-derive to check.

**What still ships, and why:** the `cr6-*`, `cr8-*`, `ice-*` corpus instances
(`knowledge/islanding/corpus/*.json`) — Max-Cut graphs with integer edge
weights, plus an embedded `ExternalSourceProvenance` that cites the retired
snapshots by digest, not their geodata. Same reasoning §1 already uses for
pandapower: what ships is a derived graph, not the substation and line
geometry it came from. These instances are NOT published as a dataset either
— the deployment catalog (`distributions/chimera/distribution.yaml`,
`GET /datasets`) declares only corpora this project generated itself, per the
2026-08-08 decision that this was example data for a single challenge and the
platform does not depend on it — but publishing the *repository* still
redistributes whatever is in its tree, and these instances are in it.

They stay for a concrete reason, and deleting them was explicitly rejected:
`knowledge/nexus/` — the real Quantinuum H2-1LE run evidence, the strongest
evidence this project has — is anchored to the `cr6-*` / `cr8-*` instances
(`index.json`, `consensus.json` reference them, never the raw geojson).
Deleting the derived instances would orphan that evidence.

**What ships as TEST material, and the same open question there:**
`capabilities/ingesta/tests/fixtures/ice-subestaciones.geojson` and
`ice-lineas-transmision.geojson` are a byte-identical copy of the same two
portal datasets (same SHA-256 as the retired raw copy), kept specifically as
fixture data for `capabilities/ingesta/tests/test_geojson_to_graph.py` —
Dylan explicitly authorized this use as test material. Same publisher, same
"public access, no stated license" status, same unresolved redistribution
question as above: attribution here credits ICE; it does not resolve whether
redistributing these bytes — even as test fixtures — is licensed. This is not
papered over: the open question stated at the top of this section applies to
this fixture exactly as it applied to the raw copy before removal.

Tracked as part of backlog item O4 (`docs/mejorado/04-consolidacion.md`), and
listed in `docs/pre-flip-checklist.md` §4.2.

